,title,link,text,language,family
0,Ramu–Lower Sepik languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramu–Lower_Sepik_languages,"July 19, 2024 - The family was proposed by William A. Foley and accepted by Malcolm Ross. Its two branches, Ramu and Lower Sepik, had belonged to Donald Laycock's now-defunct 1973 Sepik–Ramu proposal. If related, they are not close.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
1,Lower Sepik languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Sepik_languages,"January 1, 2026 - The Lower Sepik a.k.a. Nor–Pondo languages are a small language family of East Sepik Province in northern Papua New Guinea. They were identified as a family by K Laumann in 1951 under the name Nor–Pondo, and included in Donald Laycock's now-defunct 1973 Sepik–Ramu family.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
2,Sepik–Ramu languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sepik–Ramu_languages,"November 10, 2024 - The Sepik–Ramu languages are an obsolete language family of New Guinea linking the Sepik, Ramu, Nor–Pondo (Lower Sepik), Leonhard Schultze (Walio–Papi) and Yuat families, together with the Taiap language isolate, and proposed by Donald Laycock and John Z'graggen in 1975.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
3,Sepik Songs - YouTube Music,https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLHjvmRccHaa6SWHY8bqN2ZJd-g7NUPLmH,"Listen to Sepik Songs on YouTube Music - a dedicated music app with official songs, music videos, remixes, covers, and more.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
4,Sepik Hits - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL9rH6idp8uqzeg9m4Q_0ju4OXy1Rc4mpl,FELIX YAUSI (PNG Music 2019),Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
5,"Kanggape Language (IGM) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/igm/,"Kanggape is a stable indigenous language of Papua New Guinea. It belongs to the Ramu-Lower Sepik language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is not known to be taught in schools.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
6,Music and Ancestors in Papua New Guinea - Pacific Asia Museum,https://pacificasiamuseum.usc.edu/audio-tours/music-and-ancestors-in-papua-new-guinea/,"Music has been an integral part of ceremonies and celebrations in the Sepik region. Percussion and wind instruments were used to invite the attendees’ attention and to accompany dancers, but more importantly to embody the voices of ancestral spirits.",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
7,Lower Sepik Kundu drum | Lower Ramu Kundu drum,https://www.new-guinea-tribal-arts.com/tribal-art-gallery/lower-sepik-kundu-drum/,This old Lower Sepik Kundu drum is one of the best examples i have ever collected. The detail of the carving leaves no doubt,Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
8,Ramu-Lower Sepik languages - Sorosoro Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/ramu-lower-sepik-languages/,"June 7, 2010 - Lower-Sepik Branch · Murik · Kopar · Pondo sub-group · Angoram · Chambri · Tabriak · Yimas · Ramu Branch · Lower Ramu · Ottilien sub-group · Mbore · Awar · Bosmun · Kayan · Marangis · Misegian or Mikarew sub group · Aruamu · Kire · Sepen · Keram or Grass sub-group · Abu · Ambakich · Banaro · Gorovu · Ataitan or Tanggu sub-group · Andarum · Kanggape ·",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
9,garamut drum sepik ramu area new guinea,https://drummuseum.com/New-Guinea/sepik-ramu-area-garamut-drum-new-guinea.html,"Informations about garamut drums ... slit-gong in Tangu, New Guinea. Ethnos 24.pp.136-150 Chenoweth, Vida (1976) : Musical Instruments of Papua New Guinea Craig, Barry : Slit Gongs of the Sepik and Madang Provinces....",Kanggape,Ramu-Lower Sepik
